---
title: 'Background Regions'
description: 'Shade a rectangular area of the $framework Chart series area, bounded by value ranges on the x and y axes. Add labels and customise fills and strokes.'
enterprise: true
---

<!-- cspell:ignore Adelie -->

Background Regions shade a rectangular area of the series area, bounded by value ranges on the x and y axes.

Use them to highlight a zone of the chart, such as a target range or a period of interest. For a band along a single
axis, use [Cross Lines](./axes-cross-lines/); for shapes users can draw and move, use [Annotations](./annotations/).

## Adding Background Regions

Add Background Regions with the `seriesArea.backgroundRegions` array. Each region is bounded by an `xRange` and a
`yRange`.

{% chartExampleRunner title="Simple Background Region" name="simple-background-regions" type="generated" /%}

```js format="snippet"
{
    seriesArea: {
        backgroundRegions: [
            {
                xRange: { start: new Date(2025, 5, 1), end: new Date(2025, 8, 1) },
                yRange: { start: 0, end: 50 },
                label: {
                    text: 'Drought Risk',
                },
            },
        ],
    },
}
```

In this configuration:

- `xRange` and `yRange` bound the region with `start` and `end` values, given in the units of the axis they apply to.
- `label.text` adds a label to the region.

Regions are drawn behind the series, above the chart background. Region labels are drawn in front of the series.

{% note %}
Background Regions are only supported on cartesian charts.
{% /note %}

## Range Bounds

Every part of `xRange` and `yRange` is optional. Omitting a `start` or an `end` extends that side of the region to the
edge of the series area, and omitting a range entirely spans the region across that axis.

{% chartExampleRunner title="Range Bounds" name="range-bounds" type="generated" /%}

```js format="snippet"
{
    seriesArea: {
        backgroundRegions: [
            {
                xRange: { start: new Date(2025, 5, 1) },
                yRange: { end: 50 },
                label: {
                    text: 'Drought Risk',
                },
            },
        ],
    },
}
```

In this configuration:

- `xRange` has no `end`, so the region extends to the right edge of the series area.
- `yRange` has no `start`, so it extends to the bottom edge.
- Use the buttons to switch between these bounds, a region bounded on both axes, and one with `xRange` omitted
  altogether so that it spans the full width.
- Values outside the axis domain are clamped to the edge of the series area.
- If `start` is greater than `end`, the region renders as if the two values were swapped.

## Labels

Use `label.position` to place a label relative to its region. Labels are styled with the usual font, colour, padding
and border options.

{% chartExampleRunner title="Labels" name="region-labels" type="generated" /%}

```js format="snippet"
{
    label: {
        text: 'Drought Risk',
        position: 'inside-top-left',
    },
}
```

In this example:

- Use the dropdown to change `position`.
- Positions prefixed with `inside` place the label within the region; the others place it outside the corresponding
  edge or corner.
- `position` defaults to `'top'`.

## Multiple Axes

On a chart with [multiple axes](./axes-secondary/) in one direction, use `axis` to name the axis a range applies to.

{% chartExampleRunner title="Multiple Axes" name="multiple-axes" type="generated" /%}

```js format="snippet"
{
    seriesArea: {
        backgroundRegions: [
            {
                yRange: { axis: 'rainfall', start: 100 },
                label: {
                    text: 'Heavy Rainfall',
                    position: 'inside-top-left',
                },
            },
        ],
    },
    axes: {
        x: { type: 'unit-time' },
        y: { type: 'number' },
        rainfall: { type: 'number', position: 'right' },
    },
}
```

In this configuration:

- `yRange.axis` is set to `'rainfall'`, so the range is resolved against that axis rather than the primary y-axis.
- `xRange` is omitted, so the region spans the full width of the series area.
- When `axis` is omitted, the range applies to the first axis declared in that direction.

## Customisation

Regions are styled with `fill`, `fillOpacity`, `stroke`, `strokeWidth` and `strokeOpacity`. Labels have their own
text, box and border options.

{% chartExampleRunner title="Customisation" name="customisation" type="generated" /%}

```js format="snippet"
{
    seriesArea: {
        backgroundRegions: [
            {
                fill: '#5090dc',
                fillOpacity: 0.2,
                stroke: '#2b5c95',
                strokeWidth: 2,
                xRange: percentileRange(flipperLengths),
                yRange: percentileRange(bodyMasses),
                label: {
                    text: 'Adelie',
                    position: 'inside-top',
                    color: '#2b5c95',
                    fontSize: 13,
                    fontWeight: 'bold',
                    fill: '#ffffff',
                    fillOpacity: 0.85,
                    cornerRadius: 4,
                    padding: { top: 4, right: 8, bottom: 4, left: 8 },
                    border: {
                        enabled: true,
                        stroke: '#2b5c95',
                    },
                },
            },
        ],
    },
}
```

In this example:

- Each species has a region covering the middle 80% of its measurements.
- Each label is colour-matched to its region, with `fill`, `cornerRadius`, `padding` and `border` styling the box
  behind the text and `color` styling the text itself.
- Where `fill` is omitted, regions use the theme's default fill. A `stroke` is only drawn when one is specified.

